<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hiraga Gennai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1728–1780</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hiraga Gennai was a Japanese scholar of Western learning, inventor, writer and painter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, born in Sanuki province. He built an electrostatic generator, promoted mining and natural history, and wrote popular fiction and plays. Visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akita">Akita</a> in 1773 to advise on its mines, he taught Western painting techniques to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odano-naotake">Odano Naotake</a>, giving rise to the Akita school of painting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hiraga-gennai.jpg" alt="Hiraga Gennai" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 木村黙老 / Momuō Kimura · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Portrait_of_Ky%C5%ABkei_Hiraga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
